### Executive Summary & Context: Where Existing Tokio Already Steals vs. What Current Data Shows

Before ranking new experiments, we must separate what multi-thread Tokio (`Runtime::new_multi_thread`) **already does** from what the current experimental modes in [`rt/src/tasks/experimental.rs`](file:///private/tmp/spawned-fede-next/rt/src/tasks/experimental.rs) and [`concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs`](file:///private/tmp/spawned-fede-next/concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs) test:

1. **Where Existing Multi-Thread Tokio Already Does Work Stealing**:
   - In the default path ([`ActorRef::spawn`](file:///private/tmp/spawned-fede-next/concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs#L556-L645)), every `Backend::Async` actor is a single long-lived Tokio task executing [`run_actor`](file:///private/tmp/spawned-fede-next/concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs#L702-L726) / [`run_actor_lifecycle`](file:///private/tmp/spawned-fede-next/concurrency/src/tasks/actor.rs#L728-L814).
   - Multi-thread Tokio already maintains per-worker LIFO slots + 256-slot local run queues, cross-worker work-stealing, a global injection queue for external wakes, and a 128-poll cooperative budget per tick.
   - **Consequence on 8-node graphs (`chain` & `fanout`)**: Even when actors are spawned in nominal `cross` order in [`layout()`](file:///private/tmp/spawned-fede-next/concurrency/benches/workload_research.rs#L249-L289), when parent [`Node::handle`](file:///private/tmp/spawned-fede-next/concurrency/benches/workload_research.rs#L211-L248) sends a `Route` request to a child, Tokio wakes the child task into the waking worker's LIFO slot / local queue (or steals it onto an idle worker). Thus Tokio dynamically co-locates active graph edges (~951k root/s `chain` across both nominal placements; ~952k `fanout` on short 5-rep runs, pending long confirmations).
   - **Consequence under concentrated hot/cold overload**: When hot actors monopolize a worker, other Tokio workers steal cold actors off that worker's local queue, preventing permanent `shard0` head-of-line pinning.

2. **Why Current Static `sharded` (`Pool`) Wins Independent RPC / `same` Graphs Today, and Loses on `cross` Graphs / `shard0` Overload**:
   - [`Pool::spawn`](file:///private/tmp/spawned-fede-next/rt/src/tasks/experimental.rs#L91-L112) permanently pins each actor via blind global round-robin (`self.next.fetch_add(1)`) onto one of $N$ isolated `new_current_thread()` Tokio runtimes (`spawned-shard-{id}`).
   - **Independent RPC & `same`-shard graphs**: Zero cross-worker steal scanning, zero task migration, and cache-hot per-shard reactors yield higher single-hop throughput (2.070M/s vs 1.777M/s on old frozen 9b) and parity on `same` fanout (~952k) / near-parity on `same` chain (~864k).
   - **`cross`-shard graphs (~514k chain, ~630k fanout)**: Every parent $\to$ child hop in [`Node::handle`](file:///private/tmp/spawned-fede-next/concurrency/benches/workload_research.rs#L219-L233) crosses pinned `current_thread` runtimes, forcing cross-thread `mpsc`/`oneshot` wakeups (`eventfd`/`epoll` unparks and context switches) with no way to migrate or steal the child onto the parent's worker.
   - **Concentrated overload**: Cold actors statically assigned to `shard0` cannot escape the hot worker, inflating `cold_shard0_observed_p99_ns`, `worst_cold_actor_observed_p99_ns`, and `cold_late_observed`.

